==Production==
===Development===
In February 2019, [[Marvel Television]] announced plans to develop an adult animated television series based on [[MODOK]], with a series order at [[Hulu]], along with ones based on [[Hit-Monkey]], [[Tigra]] and [[Dazzler (Marvel Comics)|Dazzler]], and [[Howard the Duck]], that were intended to lead up to a crossover special titled ''The Offenders''. The series was created by Jordan Blum and [[Patton Oswalt]], both of whom were expected to write for the series and executive produce alongside [[Jeph Loeb]].<ref name="SeriesOrderVariety" /><ref name="SeriesOrderDeadline" /><ref name="SeriesOrderTHR" /> Brett Crawley, Robert Maitia, Grant Gish, [[Joe Quesada]], and [[Karim Zreik]] also executive produce.<ref name="MarvelReleaseDate" /> In December, Marvel Television was folded into [[Marvel Studios]], which carried subsequent oversight of the series.<ref name="MarvelTVFolding" /> The following month, Marvel decided not to move forward with ''Howard the Duck'', ''Tigra & Dazzler'', and ''The Offenders'', with ''M.O.D.O.K.'' and ''[[Hit-Monkey (TV series)|Hit-Monkey]]'' continuing as planned.<ref name="MarvelAnimatedUpdated" />

Following Marvel Television's absorption into Marvel Studios, Blum stated that [[Kevin Feige]] was supportive of the show and told him to "keep going" with the concept. Blum was allowed to give the series its own [[Multiverse (Marvel Comics)|multiverse designation]] and chose Earth-1226 in honor of his son's birthday.<ref name="EarthDesignation" /> In April 2021, Blum revealed that a second season was planned.<ref name="MelissaBiSeason2" /> In January 2022, Hulu's head of content Craig Erwich stated that additional seasons of ''M.O.D.O.K.'' would be determined solely by the Marvel Studios team.<ref name="ErwichFutureJan2022" /> In May 2022, Hulu canceled the series after one season.<ref name="Canceled" />

===Casting===
The series' announcement revealed that Oswalt was set to voice M.O.D.O.K.<ref name="CreatorsTalk" /> In January 2020, the announcement of [[Aimee Garcia]], [[Ben Schwartz]], [[Melissa Fumero]], [[Wendi McLendon-Covey]], [[Beck Bennett]], [[Jon Daly]], and [[Sam Richardson (actor)|Sam Richardson]] as the series' cast was confirmed.<ref name="Cast" /> By then, Craig Erwich, Hulu's Senior Vice President of Originals, revealed that "a few episodes of" the series had completed recording.<ref name="EpisodesUpdate" />

=== Writing ===
By October 2020, Oswalt confirmed that writing and audio recording for the series had concluded,<ref name="OswaltOct2020Update" /> under the [[working title]] ''Bighead''.<ref name="WorkingTitle" /> Oswalt stated that Marvel allowed him and the creative team to include a variety of popular and relatively unknown characters in the series, which include several superheroes and members of [[X-Men]]–related teams.<ref name="IGNNYCCPanel" /> Blum said that due to rights issues, the only characters they could not use were [[Stilt-Man]], [[Trapster|Paste-Pot Pete]] and [[Turner D. Century]]. Prior to the cancellation of ''The Offenders'', Blum was brought on to overlook the other shows and had even thought up about what the crossover would have entailed. He added that not much had changed storywise when it was decided to make ''M.O.D.O.K.'' a standalone series.<ref name="ColliderInterview" />

===Animation===
Work on the stop-motion aspects of the series was revealed to have been completed by October 2020 with [[Stoopid Buddy Stoodios]] providing the visuals for the episodes, which had "every frame [packed] with crazy detail".<ref name="OswaltOct2020Update" /> During the series' [[New York Comic Con]] online panel later that month, Oswalt revealed that Stoopid Buddy Studios created "innovative technology" that used handheld-styled cinematography techniques for the stop-motion puppet designs of the characters and environment.<ref name="/FilmNYCCPanel" />

===Music===
[[Daniel Rojas (film composer)|Daniel Rojas]] serves as the composer for the series.<ref name="OswaltOct2020Update" />

==Marketing==
In September 2020, Hulu revealed the series' logo,<ref name="LogoReveal" /> and ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' released first look images for the series on October 9, 2020,<ref name="EWFirstLook" /> ahead of a panel that was held during the virtual [[New York Comic Con]] later that day, where Blum and Oswalt presented two clips from the series and a behind-the-scenes [[B-roll]].<ref name="SyFyWireNYCCPanel" />

To promote the series, Patton Oswalt and Jordan Blum co-wrote a miniseries for [[Marvel Comics]] titled ''[[M.O.D.O.K.: Head Games]]''. The comic quickly establishes the existence of M.O.D.O.K.'s family from the Hulu series into the mainstream Marvel Universe, albeit as a trio of Super-Adaptoids that were copied from his hallucinations brought upon by a glitched program in his head.<ref name="ComicTieIn" />

==Release==
''M.O.D.O.K.'' premiered on [[Hulu]] on May 21, 2021, releasing all ten episodes simultaneously.<ref name="MarvelReleaseDate" /> On that same day, the first episode was released on the [[Star (Disney+)|Star]] content hub of [[Disney+]] with a subsequent weekly window.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lovejoy |first=Hannah |date=April 19, 2021 |title=Lucifer and Brooklyn Nine-Nine stars' Marvel MODOK show release date confirmed by Disney+ UK |url=https://www.digitalspy.com/tv/ustv/a36161428/marvel-modok-disney-plus-uk-release-date/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210419133548/https://www.digitalspy.com/tv/ustv/a36161428/marvel-modok-disney-plus-uk-release-date/ |archive-date=April 19, 2021 |access-date=April 19, 2021 |website=[[Digital Spy]]}}</ref>

== Reception ==
=== Viewership ===
[[Whip Media]], which tracks global TV viewership habits for nearly 1 million global users of its [[TV Time]] app daily, reported that ''M.O.D.O.K.'' was the third most-anticipated new television series for May 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 29, 2021 |title='Star Wars: The Bad Batch' Top Anticipated New Show in May |url=https://www.mediaplaynews.com/star-wars-the-bad-batch-top-anticipated-new-show-in-may/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221005185133/https://www.mediaplaynews.com/star-wars-the-bad-batch-top-anticipated-new-show-in-may/ |archive-date=October 5, 2022 |access-date=October 5, 2022|website=[[Media Play News]]|last=Prange|first=Stephanie}}</ref> Market research company Parrot Analytics, which looks at consumer engagement in consumer research, streaming, downloads, and on social media, announced that ''M.O.D.O.K.'' had an audience demand multiplier of 4.6 as of May 29, 2021, meaning it was 4.6 times more in demand than the average television series.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 30, 2021 |title=Hulu Renew/Cancel: Marvel's MODOK Solid on a Platform that Doesn't Own Its Top Shows |url=http://www.thetvratingsguide.com/2021/05/hulu-renewcancel-marvels-modok-solid-on.html |access-date=2026-01-23 |website=The TV Ratings Guide}}</ref> 

=== Critical response ===
The review aggregator website [[Rotten Tomatoes]] reports an 88% approval rating with an average rating of 7.77/10 based on 34 critic reviews. The website's critical consensus reads: "Though its tendency toward too-muchness may test some viewers' patience, slick stop-motion, a killer voice cast, and a seemingly endless well of jokes make ''M.O.D.O.K.'' an entertainingly chaotic diversion."<ref>{{cite Rotten Tomatoes|title=Marvel's M.O.D.O.K.|id=marvels_modok|type=tv|season=1|access-date=May 26, 2021}}{{cbignore}}</ref> On [[Metacritic]], it has a weighted average score of 71 out of 100 based on 8 critic reviews, indicating "generally favorable reviews".<ref>{{cite Metacritic|title=Marvel's M.O.D.O.K|id=marvels-modok|type=tv|season=1|access-date=May 20, 2021}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

Glen Weldon of [[NPR]] gave the series a positive review, comparing it to ''[[Robot Chicken]]'' and stating, "''Marvel's M.O.D.O.K.'' was expressly created to cordon off one tiny, weird, backwater corner of the Marvel Universe, and go nuts. Which is exactly what it accomplishes, in a fun, if ''Robot Chicken''-flavored, way."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weldon |first=Glen |date=May 21, 2021 |title='Marvel's M.O.D.O.K.': A Goofy Supervillain Basks In A Comic Spotlight |url=https://www.npr.org/2021/05/21/998249784/marvels-m-o-d-o-k-a-goofy-supervillain-basks-in-a-comic-spotlight |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210709182053/https://www.npr.org/2021/05/21/998249784/marvels-m-o-d-o-k-a-goofy-supervillain-basks-in-a-comic-spotlight |archive-date=July 9, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |publisher=[[NPR]]}}</ref> Daniel Fienberg of ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' gave the series a more critical review, comparing it to both ''Robot Chicken'' and [[Harley Quinn (TV series)|''Harley Quinn'']], saying that it "falls short of ''Harley Quinn'' territory and lands smack-dab in the middle of ''Robot Chicken'' country," and finally stating that "The voices and pell-mell references are the biggest reason why a 10-episode weekend binge of ''Marvel's MODOK'' amounted to such easy, quickly digested entertainment. Whether or not you think the series wants to amount to more than that — and I feel like it really does, and perhaps someday could — depends on you."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fienberg |first=Daniel |date=May 19, 2021 |title='Marvel's MODOK': TV Review |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-reviews/marvels-modok-1234955228/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521151607/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-reviews/marvels-modok-1234955228/ |archive-date=May 21, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>

Eric Francisco of Inverse gave the series a positive review, stating that "In its own tragicomic corner, outside the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]], ''M.O.D.O.K.'' shines as a nuanced portrait of a [[supervillain]] trying to have it all" and that "''M.O.D.O.K.'' has strengths all its own, primarily as a too-real [[tragicomedy]] about a life gone awry and a marriage crumbling into dust," while adding that "it isn't as funny, vulgar, or insightful as ''Harley Quinn ...'' nor is it as boundary-expanding as ''[[WandaVision]]''."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Francisco |first=Eric |title=M.O.D.O.K. review: Hulu gives Marvel Studios its greatest villain to date |url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/modok-hulu-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210525181216/https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/modok-hulu-review |archive-date=May 25, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |website=[[Inverse (website)|Inverse]]|date=May 19, 2021 }}</ref> Siddhant Adlakha of [[IGN]] gave the series an 8 out of 10 rating, stating that "Marvel's farcical M.O.D.O.K. series is a refreshing, mile-a-minute comic self-parody. Every line is a laugh, and Patton Oswalt is pitch-perfect as the iconic supervillain, whose [[Frankenstein's monster|Frankenstein]] origins are traded in for a streamlined saga of ego and ambition, blended with both a modern workplace comedy and a surprisingly moving family sitcom."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Adlakha |first=Siddhant |date=May 17, 2021 |title=Marvel's M.O.D.O.K.: Season 1 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/marvel-modok-season-1-review-hulu |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210702081732/https://www.ign.com/articles/marvel-modok-season-1-review-hulu |archive-date=July 2, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref>

''[[Collider (website)|Collider]]'' included ''M.O.D.O.K'' in their "Best Comedy Shows on Hulu" list in September 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |date=September 16, 2020 |title=The Best Comedy Shows on Hulu Right Now |url=https://collider.com/best-comedy-shows-on-hulu/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221005185817/https://collider.com/best-comedy-shows-on-hulu/ |archive-date=October 5, 2022 |access-date=October 5, 2022 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref> ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' included ''M.O.D.O.K'' in their "The best TV shows on Hulu" list of Summer 2022.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last1=Cordero |first1=Rosy |last2=Lash |first2=Jolie |last3=Kinane |first3=Ruth |last4=Wang |first4=Jessica |title=The best shows on Hulu |url=https://ew.com/tv/the-best-shows-on-hulu-right-now/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221005185817/https://ew.com/tv/the-best-shows-on-hulu-right-now/ |archive-date=October 5, 2022 |access-date=October 5, 2022 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref>

=== Accolades ===
''M.O.D.O.K.'' was nominated for Best Animation TrailerByte for a Feature Film at the 2022 [[Golden Trailer Awards]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=GTA22 Nominees – Digital & Innovative Categories – Golden Trailer Awards |url=https://goldentrailer.com/gta22-nominees-digital-innovative-categories/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221023205346/https://goldentrailer.com/gta22-nominees-digital-innovative-categories/ |archive-date=October 23, 2022 |access-date=October 23, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Davis |first=Clayton |date=August 2, 2022 |title=Golden Trailer Awards: 'The Batman' and 'Top Gun: Maverick' Among Nominees, Disney Leads for Studios (EXCLUSIVE) |url=https://variety.com/2022/awards/news/golden-trailer-awards-2022-the-batman-top-gun-maverick-1235330834/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803185431/https://variety.com/2022/awards/news/golden-trailer-awards-2022-the-batman-top-gun-maverick-1235330834/ |archive-date=August 3, 2022 |access-date=October 23, 2022 |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref>

